      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "text": "The provisions governing transactions involving conflicts of interest and interest-party transactions are set out in the section of the Law dedicated to the regulation of public joint-stock companies, which, as specified therein, apply equally to private joint-stock companies.   [5] In addition, the Law establishes that its provisions concerning joint-stock companies apply to limited liability companies, unless the application of any such provisions conflicts with the legal nature of the limited liability company.   [6]   In this regard, the UAE Cabinet may issue a decision clarifying which specific provisions of the Law apply to limited liability companies and establishing rules for the application of related-party transactions to limited liability companies.   [7]   As of now, no such decision has been made.   Previously effective UAE Law No. 2 (2015)  On Commercial Companies  also contained a similar provision in Article 104 regarding the application of the rules governing joint-stock companies to limited liability companies mutatis mutandis.   To further elaborate on the provisions of this article, Ministerial Decision No. 272 (2016) ( \"Decision\" ) was adopted; the foregoing Decision clarified which provisions of the law applicable to public joint-stock companies are applicable to limited liability companies and which are not.   Among other things, the Decision stipulated that the rules governing related-party transactions do not apply to limited liability companies.   As a result, only the rules governing transactions involving conflict of interest applied to limited liability companies.   Given that the  Law on Commercial Companies  was amended in 2021 and the Decision was not formally repealed, there is currently uncertainty as to whether the Decision applies to the Law.   In the absence of official clarification, it is quite likely that the Decision continues to apply to the Law to the extent that it does not conflict with it, and, accordingly, limited liability companies remain exempt from the rules governing related-party transactions.",
      "formatted_text": [
        {
          "text": "The provisions governing transactions involving conflicts of interest and interest-party transactions are set out in the section of the Law dedicated to the regulation of public joint-stock companies, which, as specified therein, apply equally to private joint-stock companies.",
          "formatting": {
            "bold": false,
            "italic": false,
            "underline": false,
            "strike": false,
            "font_name": null,
            "font_size": null,
            "color": null,
